Sideric Swing

Ingredients: CHICAGO HOUSE & GARAGE HOUSE + CINEMATIC, EPIC & ORCHESTRAL + Xylophone  
Creative Catalyst: Its instruments are built from meteorite fragments and antique clockwork.

Sideric Swing is a relentless, high-velocity dance collision where the sweaty syncopation of warehouse house music collides with the catastrophic grandeur of a sixty-piece symphony orchestra. Rather than relying on plastic synthesizer patches or standard TR-909 percussion, its rhythmic backbone is forged from interlocking brass gear-trains, heavy pendulum escapements, and the brilliant, bell-like strike of nickel-iron meteorite bars hammered into xylophonic arrays. Swung garage hi-hats are replaced by the stuttering release of balance springs; grand, sweeping string sections swell to Wagnerian heights before being violently sidechained against meteor-core sub-kicks; and soaring, church-honed vocal toplines trade frantic riffs with shimmering, extraterrestrial mallet leads.

Genre DNA

- Tempo & Pulse: 122–126 BPM. The pulse takes Chicago’s straight four-on-the-floor kick and skews it with Garage's heavily swung, skipping 16th-note pocket, propelled not by digital quantization but by the mechanical, asymmetric inertia of antique clock escapements.
- Harmonic Palette: Lush, emotive minor 9th and 11th chords drawn from classic New Jersey and Garage house gospel traditions, transposed onto grand cinematic string chorales and brass fanfares in C minor and E-flat Dorian, complicated by the inharmonic, microtonal overtones of raw meteoric iron.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Gravitational Escapement Gating: Massive orchestral crescendos are passed through analog gates triggered mechanically by ticking grandfather clock movements, chopping symphonic grandeur into breathless, rhythmic stutter-patterns.
  2. Siderite Mallet Tracking: The central melodic hook is always played on an acoustic xylophone fabricated from Widmanstätten-pattern meteorite slabs, struck by automated brass clockwork hammers to produce a razor-sharp, otherworldly metallic resonance that pierces through wall-of-sound orchestration.
  3. Turret-Reverb Ducking: Cavernous concert-hall acoustics and plate reverbs are sidechained directly to a low-end pulse sourced from a 500-pound iron pendulum striking stone, sucking the vast cinematic space dry on every downbeat to make room for sweat-drenched dancefloor swing.
- Dynamics: Songs begin with deceptive, ticking intimacy—isolated clockworks, faint music-box chimes, and solitary mallet phrases—before detonating into tidal orchestral climaxes that suddenly drop away into stark, hyper-syncopated bass-and-xylophone jack-grooves.

Scene & Origin Lore

Sideric Swing erupted in the winter of 2028 from the subterranean vaults beneath Chicago’s historic Calumet industrial basin. A rogue collective of out-of-work master horologists and South Side house purists broke into the dormant storage facilities of the city's geological institutes, salvaging pre-industrial clock towers and iron-heavy meteorite falls. Rejecting modern electronic controllers, they built colossal hybrid instruments known as "Chrono-Stomps"—massive, gear-driven mallet sequencers powered by falling counterweights that struck tuned meteorite ingots. The scene's followers, known as Turners, dance in abandoned railway concourses wearing heavy charcoal wool waistcoats, watchmaker loupes strapped over sweatbands, and velvet track jackets dusted with fine iron shavings. Ritual gatherings feature no set times; ravers arrive when the public clocks in the venue are stopped by hand, dancing until the falling escapement weights touch the concrete floor.

Typical Instruments: Meteorite-bar xylophone (played manually and via clockwork gears), antique tower-clock escapements and gear trains (percussion), full chamber and orchestral string sections (contrabass, cello, viola, violin), French horns, brass orchestral stabs, upright bass, modified acoustic kick-drums weighted with meteorite fragments, tape-delayed soul vocalists.

